Facts:
On the night of May 24, 2003, in Barangay Palacpalac, Pozorrubio, Pangasinan, the spouses Jesus and Ana Del Mundo were inside their home when stones suddenly hit their roof. Jesus stepped outside to investigate and saw his nephew, Felix Velasquez, along with petitioners Nicolas Velasquez and Victor Velasquez, accompanied by three others. Jesus confronted them, asking why they were throwing stones.
Instead of answering, petitioner Nicolas struck Jesus on the head with a stone. An altercation followed, during which Jesus was pushed to the ground. While pinned down, Jesus was assaulted by the group: Victor held his legs, others pinned his arms, and Felix struck him on the head with a piece of wood. Ana shouted for help, prompting nearby neighbors to intervene and end the mauling. Jesus was brought to the hospital, where he was treated for head lacerations that required medical attendance for roughly 10 to 14 days.
An Information for Attempted Murder was filed against Nicolas, Victor, and their companions. In their defense, Nicolas and Victor invoked defense of a relative and defense of a stranger. They alleged that it was Jesus who initiated the violence by drunkenly brandishing a bolo (itak) and attempting to hack Felix and Ampong (a bystander/relative). Nicolas claimed he threw a stone at Jesus solely to stop him from hacking his companion, and Victor claimed he merely tried to wrestle the bolo away.
The Regional Trial Court (RTC) rejected the justifying circumstances and convicted Nicolas and Victor of Attempted Murder. The Court of Appeals (CA) affirmed the conviction.

Issue: 
Whether petitioners Nicolas Velasquez and Victor Velasquez established by clear and convincing evidence that they acted in legitimate defense of a relative or defense of a stranger under Article 11 of the Revised Penal Code.
Ruling: 
1. No. Petitioners failed to establish the indispensable element of unlawful aggression.
Whether invoking self-defense, defense of a relative (Art. 11[2]), or defense of a stranger (Art. 11[3]), unlawful aggression is the conditio sine qua non. Without an actual, sudden, and unprovoked attack by the victim, justification cannot be appreciated, complete or incomplete:
•	Failure to Prove the Existence of the Bolo: The petitioners' claim that Jesus attacked them with a bolo was uncorroborated, self-serving, and contradicted by the evidence. No bladed weapon was recovered from Jesus or found at the scene.
•	Aggression Originated from Petitioners: The credible testimony of the prosecution witnesses established that the petitioners and their group provoked the incident by pelting the victim's house with stones and then ganging up on him when he came out to inquire.
•	Because Jesus committed no act of unlawful aggression, the invocation of defense of a relative or defense of a stranger completely collapsed.

Ratio Decidendi:

Dispositive:
petitioners Nicolas Velasquez and Victor Velasquez were found guilty beyond reasonable doubt of Less Serious Physical Injuries
Question:
Explain why the Supreme Court held that the accused in these cases did not act in defense of a relative.

Answer:
Petitioners Nicolas and Victor Velasquez claimed they struck Jesus Del Mundo with stones and pinned him to protect their nephew/relative, Felix Velasquez, asserting that Jesus was a drunken aggressor charging at Felix with an itak (bolo). The Supreme Court denied their defense on the following grounds:
•	Unsubstantiated Claim of a Deadly Weapon: The defense’s narrative depended entirely on the assertion that the victim wielded a bolo. However, no bolo was ever recovered at the scene or produced in court, and medical records showed no injuries inflicted on any of the accused. A mere self-serving allegation that the victim held a weapon cannot overcome credible eyewitness accounts to the contrary.
•	The Accused Were the Initial Aggressors: Unlawful aggression must originate from the victim. Here, the accused initiated the violence by pelting stones at the victim's roof late at night. When Jesus stepped outside unarmed to inquire why his house was being stoned, the accused immediately struck him with a stone, pinned him to the ground, and beat him with a wooden stick. The victim was reacting defensively to their initial harassment.
•	Absence of Actual or Imminent Peril: Because the victim was unarmed and pinned to the ground by multiple attackers, there was never any real, positive, and imminent threat to Felix's life or limb that could legally justify hitting Jesus on the head with stones or clubs.

